rok.kralj@gmail.com wrote:

> Just recently, I started playing the game in the Terminal.
>
> In vulture eye, I immediately see the type of scroll, potion or armor. All items in my sight.
>
> After googling, I have not found it possible using the original text interface - Vulture is using special patched version of nethack that gives unfair information.
>
> What is your opinion on this subject?

Both Vulture's and all tiles ports I know of disclose item descriptions
at a distance (even when blind); ASCII doesn't, but it does disclose the
item's approximate color (which allows you to narrow down the
descriptions of potions to a few choices, for instance, but doesn't
help much with weapons and doesn't help at all with scrolls, which are
all white).

The code seems inconsistent on whether this information is meant to be
available to the player or not; I'm not sure what the original intention
was. For instance, it's revealed by blessed object detection but not
uncursed object detection, which is weird as it's revealed anyway in
tiles; and the information about an object's appearance is sent to
windowports, some of which use it, some of which don't. It's almost as
if the windowport developers and item developers have different ideas
about whether that information's meant to be player-visible or not.

The last time I discussed this on IRC, the conclusion was that the
information leaked was minor enough to usually not matter, except when
playing Zen conduct (Zen ASCII and Zen tiles are effectively different
conducts, due to description-IDing things being much easier in tiles).
Still, it'd be nice to hear from the devteam about what the actual
intention is.

(The obvious fix is to use generic tiles for items that haven't been
seen in detail, but this would need some extra code, and a bunch of
extra art for "generic potion", etc..)

